## How it works

The first four steps are the same for every repository. What happens after your first push depends on whether the repository is public or private.

<HowItWorks />

## The script is the clip

Each clip is a TypeScript file in the repository. The video beside it is what CI recorded from that file on the last push to `main`, and the highlighted line is the one running.

<Take base="{{clips}}" />

## What happens when you change the app

<Ledger />

Every push to `main` runs `npx versioncam check` against the example app. The left panel shows the latest run. The right panel shows the same check, which we ran once for this page after we renamed one button's test id and changed nothing else.

<Runs base="{{clips}}" />

## Why a recording comes out the same every time

<dl class="vc-facts not-content">
  <div>
    <dt>Authored time</dt>
    <dd>The recorder controls the page's clock and moves it one frame at a time. While a query is slow or a spinner is showing, the recorder waits with the clock stopped, so the wait never appears in the video. <a href="/how-it-works/overview/">How it works</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Real input</dt>
    <dd>The recorder moves a real mouse along a planned path, generated from a seed, so hover and press states fire as they would for a person. The cursor you see in the video is drawn afterwards, at the output resolution. <a href="/reference/dsl/">The clip DSL</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Same bytes</dt>
    <dd>Record a clip twice on one machine and you get the same file, byte for byte. Before the Claude Code skill writes a clip for your app, it runs <code>versioncam stability</code>, which records a three-second clip twice and checks that both come out the same. <a href="/how-it-works/determinism/">Determinism</a></dd>
  </div>
  <div>
    <dt>Write a clip with one sentence</dt>
    <dd>In Claude Code, <code>/versioncam "…"</code> writes the clip and records it. Then a separate reviewer agent checks the frames, and each round gets a new reviewer. You don't need an API key: it uses the Claude Code session you already have. <a href="/start/authoring/">Authoring</a></dd>
  </div>
</dl>

## After CI: version.cam

The recorder and the GitHub Action are free. They run in your CI, which already has your app and your secrets. version.cam is where the recordings go after CI has made them.

<Hosted />

## Set up

You run three commands, then give Claude Code one sentence. The first time the skill runs, it writes the config from what it finds in the repository, checks that the app records the same way twice, and writes the first clip. On the example app, that first run took [six to twelve minutes and one or two review rounds](/start/authoring/).

